{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "composite": true,
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "module": "ESNext",
    "moduleResolution": "Bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "strict": true
  },
  // The browser suite reads the scenario's own numbers, so the domain it imports is
  // part of this project too — and so is the headless run harness, which is how a domain
  // test gets a log that a real student could have produced rather than one it typed.
  //
  // `labels.ts` is here for the same reason as `choices.ts`: it is the one table of words the
  // educator surface renders, and a browser test that spelled a level itself would pass while
  // the screen said something else. Reading it is what let the golden journeys survive the
  // vocabulary rename without a single assertion being edited.
  //
  // `fixtures/demoClass.ts` is here for the inverse reason. The pilot rehearsal's job is partly
  // to prove no fixture ever reaches a real class, and it checked that by naming three strings
  // the sample class used to contain — every one of which the sample class stopped containing
  // when it was rebuilt, leaving three assertions that were green against a page leaking the
  // fixture and green against a blank page equally. A "this must not appear" test is only worth
  // anything when the thing it names is read from whatever would produce it.
  //
  // `content/studentCopy.ts` joins them for the student half of the same argument. The world
  // picker's title lives there, and `flow.ts` had it written out as `/Pick a world/i` — a
  // regex over a sentence the product owns, in the one helper every student journey passes
  // through. It happens to still match; the four educator literals this file's siblings were
  // rewritten for happened to stop matching, and there is no way to tell which kind you have
  // written except by reading the string from the place that renders it.
  //
  // `chapters.ts` and the two files it reaches are the newest members, and they join for the
  // reason the paragraph above gives. The golden-journey spec asserts that a student who stops
  // mid-run comes back to the place they stopped, and the words for that place are computed by
  // `chapters.ts` — `stepPosition` and `stoppedAt`. A browser test that spelled the resume
  // label itself would be asserting its own copy of a sentence the product owns, which is the
  // exact failure mode `studentCopy.ts` was added here to close.
  //
  // Three names rather than `src/student/**/*.ts`, and the difference is not style. The glob
  // drags in `reading/index.ts`, which re-exports a `.tsx` this project has no `--jsx` for, and
  // `homeModel.ts`, which reaches back out into `src/stages/`. A browser project that compiles
  // the student view layer is a browser project that fails for reasons that have nothing to do
  // with the browser.
  //
  // `visual/**` is the craft suite: a second browser project that asks what is painted on top of
  // what. It reads `e2e/flow.ts` and the scenario numbers like the assertion suite does, so it
  // belongs to the same project rather than to a tsconfig of its own.
  "include": ["vite.config.ts", "vitest.config.ts", "playwright.config.ts", "visual/**/*.ts", "eslint.config.js", "e2e/**/*.ts", "server/**/*.ts", "api/**/*.ts", "src/domain/**/*.ts", "src/platform/**/*.ts", "src/test/**/*.ts", "src/components/financial/choices.ts", "src/content/studentCopy.ts", "src/educator/labels.ts", "src/fixtures/demoClass.ts", "src/student/chapters.ts", "src/student/completedRun.ts", "src/student/session.ts"]
}
